WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Which molecule turns the calcium signal into release at this synapse?

2
00:00:07.483 --> 00:00:10.583
When an inner hair cell depolarizes, calcium enters.

3
00:00:11.583 --> 00:00:14.163
Something must turn that signal into vesicle fusion.

4
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:16.513
The ribbon is not that something.

5
00:00:17.353 --> 00:00:19.593
It keeps a larger resident pool of vesicles.

6
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:26.413
Mutations in the OTOF gene cause deafness, and otoferlin carries calcium-binding C2

7
00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:27.413
domains.

8
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:32.193
Roux and colleagues recorded from inner hair cells of mice lacking otoferlin.

9
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:37.097
A rise in membrane capacitance tracks how much vesicle membrane fused.

10
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:38.897
So it measures release.

11
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:44.727
If otoferlin acts after calcium enters, at fusion itself, what should the recording

12
00:00:44.727 --> 00:00:45.727
show?

13
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.260
Calcium current and ribbon morphogenesis were preserved at the age recorded.

14
00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.460
Release was almost abolished.

15
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.660
They proposed that otoferlin may be the major calcium sensor for fusion.

16
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.560
The word may mattered.

17
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.090
A knockout shows a protein is needed.

18
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.870
It does not show the protein is the sensor.

19
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:13.113
Michalski and colleagues made a sharper test.

20
00:01:13.913 --> 00:01:17.113
A change in the C2C domain reduced calcium binding.

21
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:23.083
The calcium sensitivity of release fell, which fits a sensing role, without making

22
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.583
otoferlin the only sensor.

23
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:30.963
Now the repair. In severe otoferlin deafness, the inner hair cells are there to

24
00:01:30.963 --> 00:01:33.843
treat, but their synapses release almost nothing.

25
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:37.923
The outer hair cells still amplify, at least early in life.

26
00:01:38.823 --> 00:01:41.923
The ear's own faint sounds, otoacoustic emissions, show it.

27
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.573
But the gene is too large for its vector.

28
00:01:45.453 --> 00:01:49.553
A conventional adeno-associated virus carries about five kilobases.

29
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:55.397
The otoferlin coding sequence is about six kilobases, before a promoter is added.

30
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.870
So Al-Moyed and colleagues split it between two vectors.

31
00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.190
A cell receiving both can rebuild the full gene.

32
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.620
If each vector independently reaches seventy percent of hair cells, what fraction

33
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.620
can make the protein?

34
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.753
About half, because a cell needs both halves to rebuild the gene.

35
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.467
The real mice are a separate question.

36
00:02:21.427 --> 00:02:24.507
Full-length otoferlin appeared in up to half their inner hair cells.

37
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.210
Fast release came back in those cells.

38
00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:31.430
Vesicle replenishment reached only part of wild-type values.

39
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.950
Treated mice had click thresholds of 40 to 60 dB SPL.

40
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.970
That is partial rescue, not normal hearing.

41
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:44.133
Replacing a missing link can work because the cells that need it are still alive.
